A||N||8||johnmac||White Letter Tires Paint||||7||
Z||000000||johnmac||03-04-2001||06:32 PM||||What's a good paint for the White Lettering of the tires?<P>Something that will hold on to rubber and be flexible.<P>Any Comments?<P>Johnmac||||reg||7
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-04-2001||06:54 PM||||Are you going to be adding your own designs to the tires, or painting the raised letters that arent white so they look like whitewalls?<P>Most tires have an all black side and a white letter side, look on the inside face of your tire and see if there's some white lettering in there...<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnmac||03-05-2001||07:34 AM||||I be painting the raised letters on the tires.<P>Johnmac||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-05-2001||04:52 PM||||PLEASE DONT!!!!!! blk looks fine, and youll have to touch them up every week. There used to be a special paint, but dont think its around anymore. You might be able to find Tire Paint Sticks if your determined in auto parts store. They look like thick crayons about 6 inches long.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||03-06-2001||10:35 AM||||Use hobby paint.  Comes in a tube/applicator (kinda looks like a big magic marker).  I used to get them at Walmart.  They come in different colors, too.  I used to use them when I showed my trucks (18 wheelers).  Even with the heat and miles my trucks would get (around 4000 miles/week), I wouldn't have to touch them up for a couple weeks at a time.<P>If you have a big truck chrome shop around, you might ask them if they either stock the paint or can order it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||03-06-2001||10:59 AM||||Hobby paint doesn't usually dry on rubber. I know this from experience. Stuff like testors , Tamiya and other such brands will either stay sticky or just crumble off.<P>I forget the name of it , but it comes in a small metal container , the diameter of a silver dollar, the name starts with an H and it works quite well.<P><BR>JM<BR>Worked at a hobby store for quite a few years......||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||03-06-2001||11:08 AM||||u can mix a small amount of talcum powder to paint and it will dry on rubber.||||reg||1
Z||000007||gixxer||03-06-2001||09:16 PM||||Slammed Fiero-<BR>Humbrol?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||03-06-2001||11:56 PM||||HUMBROL!! thats it!!... I wasn't sureif it was still available or not. We had a lot of guys into model cars , they also had the usual muscle cars , they bought it quite often for touch ups on their white letter tires..<P><BR>JM ||||reg||1
